             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: A shape is identified using its sides and corners, not its color, size or rotation. A closed straight-sided polygon has the same count of sides and vertices. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Activity1 asks pupils to SORT actual illustrated shape cards using a defensible attribute; Activity2 student chooses one authentic shape card and describes it to a partner. Several sorting categories may be valid. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Teacher checks original card shapes, student grouping and oral attributes. No invented source polygon-image labels.",
