        new(83,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L02","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-2/preparation.html","b13ea7b593153778a94d099465a30b96153cd9c5bdb29fadabd711498e1e9d7e",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.1"],
            ["Learning goal: You will recognize and draw shapes that have a specified number of sides or corners.\nIn previous lessons, you sorted and identified two-dimensional shapes based on your sides and corners. In previous grades, you built and drew squares, circles, triangles, rectangles, and hexagons. In this lesson, you recognize and draw shapes based on a given number of sides or corners.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Recognize and draw triangles, quadrilaterals, pentagons, and hexagons\n- In previous lessons, you sorted and identified two-dimensional shapes based on your sides and corners\n- Choose a representation that matches the quantities and relationship, then use it to justify and check the result.",
             "Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C D Caption: D\nApproach: “Which of these shapes are pentagons? Explain.” (A, C, and D, because you each have 5 sides and 5 corners.) “Why is B not a pentagon?” (It isn’t closed. It only has 4 corners.)\nExample 2: Complete the shape to make a quadrilateral. Then draw a different four-sided shape. Complete the shape to make a pentagon. Then draw a different five-sided shape. Complete the shape to make a hexagon. Then draw a different six-sided shape. Compare your shapes with your partner’s shapes. Find one way your shapes are the same and one way they are different.\nApproach: “What are the ways these shapes are different?” (different side lengths, different corners) “Would any of these shapes be a quadrilateral, pentagon, or hexagon? Why or why not?” (Sample response: No. The one on the far left has 4 sides and only 3 corners, so it’s not a quadrilateral.)",
             "Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.",
             "Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.",
             "You will recognize and draw shapes that have a specified number of sides or corners. In previous lessons, you sorted and identified two-dimensional shapes based on your sides and corners."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson2 Draw Shapes PLC Activity. A polygon must be closed with straight sides. Triangle3, quadrilateral4, pentagon5, hexagon6. Four or five segments alone do not ensure a closed polygon. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original warmup Which One Doesn't Belong: images A,C,D are pentagons; B is not closed. Students complete publisher partial shapes into quadrilateral, pentagon, hexagon, and draw a different correct instance of each. Educator reviews actual publisher fragments, completed pencil drawings, closure and unique valid shapes, and partner comparisons.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: A polygon must be closed with straight sides. Triangle3, quadrilateral4, pentagon5, hexagon6. Four or five segments alone do not ensure a closed polygon. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.G.A.1. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original warmup Which One Doesn't Belong: images A,C,D are pentagons; B is not closed. Students complete publisher partial shapes into quadrilateral, pentagon, hexagon, and draw a different correct instance of each. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: A polygon must be closed with straight sides. Triangle3, quadrilateral4, pentagon5, hexagon6. Four or five segments alone do not ensure a closed polygon. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original warmup Which One Doesn't Belong: images A,C,D are pentagons; B is not closed. Students complete publisher partial shapes into quadrilateral, pentagon, hexagon, and draw a different correct instance of each. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Educator reviews actual publisher fragments, completed pencil drawings, closure and unique valid shapes, and partner comparisons.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. A polygon must be closed with straight sides. Triangle3, quadrilateral4, pentagon5, hexagon6. Four or five segments alone do not ensure a closed polygon. Academic safeguard: Educator reviews actual publisher fragments, completed pencil drawings, closure and unique valid shapes, and partner comparisons.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson2: A polygon must be closed with straight sides. Triangle3, quadrilateral4, pentagon5, hexagon6. Four or five segments alone do not ensure a closed polygon. Educator reviews actual publisher fragments, completed pencil drawings, closure and unique valid shapes, and partner comparisons."]),
        new(84,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L03","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-3/preparation.html","940b693c00be0380fb8449140b2648a3aa593291b5894f89643fcd9f7eb15c91",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.1","CCSS:2.MD.A.1"],
            ["Learning goal: You will recognize and draw shapes that have sides with a given length.\nIn a previous unit, you measured and compared the length of the sides of triangles and rectangles with rulers. In previous lessons, you recognized and drew shapes on dot paper based on a given number of sides and corners. In this lesson, you measure sides with rulers to find shapes with specific attributes.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Use a ruler to draw shapes with specified side lengths, and identify the attributes of these shapes\n- In a previous unit, you measured and compared the length of the sides of triangles and rectangles with rulers\n- Use mathematical attributes such as sides, angles, parallelism, or length—not size or orientation—to justify a classification.",
             "Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C D Caption: D\nApproach: Identify the relevant attributes of the figure and use those attributes—not size or orientation—to justify the answer.\nExample 2: Diego drew a shape that has fewer than 5 sides. Two sides are 3 centimeters long. Circle shapes that could be Diego’s shape. Tyler drew a shape that has 4 sides. Each side is 2 inches long. Circle shapes that could be Tyler’s shape. Draw another shape that could be Tyler’s shape. Priya drew a shape that has more sides than Tyler’s shape. Only one side of...\nApproach: “Why were we able to draw so many different shapes even though you had to use a certain length?” (Only one side had to be 2 inches. We could draw shapes with different numbers of sides.)",
             "Step 1: Identify the figure and the attributes named in the task.\nStep 2: Measure, count, or compare sides, angles, corners, or other relevant features.\nStep 3: Use the attributes to classify, construct, or reason about the figure.\nStep 4: Explain which attributes support the result.\nStep 5: Check that the conclusion does not depend only on size or orientation.",
             "Common mistake: classifying a figure by appearance, size, or orientation. Use its mathematical attributes instead.",
             "You will recognize and draw shapes that have sides with a given length. In a previous unit, you measured and compared the length of the sides of triangles and rectangles with rulers."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson3 Specific Side Lengths. Use a real ruler and specified length units (centimeter versus inch). A four-sided shape with ALL four sides2in has perimeter8in, but is not necessarily a square. A triangle with two3cm sides may have many valid third lengths. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original Diego fewer than5 sides with TWO3cm sides; Tyler 4 sides with EACH2 inches; Priya more than4 sides and only ONE2-inch side. Original visual choice pictures and independently drawn constructions must be inspected. Teacher examines measured lengths and true original figures, and ensures cm and inches are never confused.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: Use a real ruler and specified length units (centimeter versus inch). A four-sided shape with ALL four sides2in has perimeter8in, but is not necessarily a square. A triangle with two3cm sides may have many valid third lengths. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.G.A.1, CCSS:2.MD.A.1. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original Diego fewer than5 sides with TWO3cm sides; Tyler 4 sides with EACH2 inches; Priya more than4 sides and only ONE2-inch side. Original visual choice pictures and independently drawn constructions must be inspected. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: Use a real ruler and specified length units (centimeter versus inch). A four-sided shape with ALL four sides2in has perimeter8in, but is not necessarily a square. A triangle with two3cm sides may have many valid third lengths. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Diego fewer than5 sides with TWO3cm sides; Tyler 4 sides with EACH2 inches; Priya more than4 sides and only ONE2-inch side. Original visual choice pictures and independently drawn constructions must be inspected. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Teacher examines measured lengths and true original figures, and ensures cm and inches are never confused.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. Use a real ruler and specified length units (centimeter versus inch). A four-sided shape with ALL four sides2in has perimeter8in, but is not necessarily a square. A triangle with two3cm sides may have many valid third lengths. Academic safeguard: Teacher examines measured lengths and true original figures, and ensures cm and inches are never confused.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson3: Use a real ruler and specified length units (centimeter versus inch). A four-sided shape with ALL four sides2in has perimeter8in, but is not necessarily a square. A triangle with two3cm sides may have many valid third lengths. Teacher examines measured lengths and true original figures, and ensures cm and inches are never confused."]),
        new(85,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L04","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-4/preparation.html","0851052ae38319d0326047b827c73c4d6748404be87292e2f70bde612ea0088e",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.1"],
