        new(87,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L06","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-6/preparation.html","a6d43e4a5c2324e6571857d05e492091fc735c487825c4a4a9e58af49ef0e347",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.1"],
            ["Learning goal: You will compose shapes and to recognize shapes that are made up of equal-size shapes.\nIn previous grades, you learned that shapes can be made up of other shapes. In previous lessons, you identified shapes based on the number of sides and corners and drew shapes having specific attributes. In this lesson, you continue to practice identifying shapes based on your attributes using the vocabulary from previous lessons.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Compose new shapes from equal-size smaller shapes, and identify the shapes\n- In previous grades, you learned that shapes can be made up of other shapes\n- Compare actual values or measured attributes, not the visual size or position of a drawing.",
             "Example 1: What is the same? What is different? Compose 3 different shapes using 2, 3, or 4 of the same equal-size shape. Show the outline of each block on the grid paper. Name each shape and explain how you composed it. I used ___ to compose a ___ . I used ___ to compose a ___ . I used ___ to compose a ___ .\nApproach: “Describe how these shapes are composed.” (You used 2 trapezoids to compose a hexagon. You used 4 small squares to compose a larger square.)",
             "Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.",
             "Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.",
             "You will compose shapes and to recognize shapes that are made up of equal-size shapes. In previous grades, you learned that shapes can be made up of other shapes."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson6 Compose and Decompose Shapes. Compose large shapes from two, three or four equal-size pattern blocks; composite perimeter depends on arrangement and shared edges, so counts of component sides alone do not determine outer sides. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original Activity1 asks to form THREE different shapes using equal-size blocks, trace their outlines on source GRID and name each composition. Example:2 trapezoids form a hexagon;4 small squares form a larger square. Teacher evaluates actual spatial composition, correct traced outer outline, equal-size component blocks and naming.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: Compose large shapes from two, three or four equal-size pattern blocks; composite perimeter depends on arrangement and shared edges, so counts of component sides alone do not determine outer sides. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.G.A.1. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original Activity1 asks to form THREE different shapes using equal-size blocks, trace their outlines on source GRID and name each composition. Example:2 trapezoids form a hexagon;4 small squares form a larger square. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: Compose large shapes from two, three or four equal-size pattern blocks; composite perimeter depends on arrangement and shared edges, so counts of component sides alone do not determine outer sides. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Activity1 asks to form THREE different shapes using equal-size blocks, trace their outlines on source GRID and name each composition. Example:2 trapezoids form a hexagon;4 small squares form a larger square. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Teacher evaluates actual spatial composition, correct traced outer outline, equal-size component blocks and naming.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. Compose large shapes from two, three or four equal-size pattern blocks; composite perimeter depends on arrangement and shared edges, so counts of component sides alone do not determine outer sides. Academic safeguard: Teacher evaluates actual spatial composition, correct traced outer outline, equal-size component blocks and naming.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson6: Compose large shapes from two, three or four equal-size pattern blocks; composite perimeter depends on arrangement and shared edges, so counts of component sides alone do not determine outer sides. Teacher evaluates actual spatial composition, correct traced outer outline, equal-size component blocks and naming."]),
        new(88,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L07","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-7/preparation.html","44bc4f51d84f720f52a10e6b0751a03677bfc8a23964df62b0d5fa1fa092d3f2",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.3"],
            ["Learning goal: You will recognize halves, thirds, and fourths, understanding that these terms describe equal pieces of the same whole.\nIn grade 1, you learned that when a shape is partitioned into 2 equal pieces, the pieces are called halves. You learned that when a shape is partitioned into 4 equal pieces, each piece is called a fourth or a quarter. In this lesson, you learn that when a shape is partitioned into 3 equal pieces, the pieces are called thirds.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Partition circles and rectangles into halves, thirds, and fourths, and describe the pieces\n- In grade 1, you learned that when a shape is partitioned into 2 equal pieces, the pieces are called halves\n- Use mathematical attributes such as sides, angles, parallelism, or length—not size or orientation—to justify a classification.",
             "Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C D Caption: D\nApproach: Represent both quantities, compare their values, and state the relationship using words, symbols, or a clear explanation.\nExample 2: Fold the rectangle to make 2 equal pieces and cut them out. Each piece is called a ___ . Compare with your partner. Tell how you know the pieces are equal. Fold the rectangle to make 4 equal pieces and cut them out. Each piece is called a ___ . Compare with your partner. Tell how you know the pieces are equal. Fold the rectangle to make 3 equal pieces...\nApproach: “What went wrong when I tried to partition this rectangle into thirds?” (It was hard to fold thirds because you can’t just fold it in the middle.)",
             "Step 1: Identify the quantities or expressions to compare.\nStep 2: Represent or calculate each value accurately.\nStep 3: Decide whether one is greater, less, or equal.\nStep 4: Write or explain the comparison.\nStep 5: Recheck both values before finalizing.",
             "Common mistake: deciding from appearance instead of actual values. Represent or calculate both quantities before comparing.",
             "You will recognize halves, thirds, and fourths, understanding that these terms describe equal pieces of the same whole. In grade 1, you learned that when a shape is partitioned into 2 equal pieces, the pieces are called halves."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson7 Make Halves, Thirds, and Fourths PLC Activity. A half is ONE of2 equal-area pieces, a third ONE of3 equal-area pieces, and a fourth ONE of4 equal-area pieces. Different shapes are acceptable when each SHARE has equal area. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original Activity1 learners FOLD rectangular paper into2/4/3 EQUAL-area pieces, CUT, label pieces and compare with partner. Unequal areas are not thirds even if three pieces exist. Source warmup figures and real folds/cuts require human equality-of-area assessment, not piece count only.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: A half is ONE of2 equal-area pieces, a third ONE of3 equal-area pieces, and a fourth ONE of4 equal-area pieces. Different shapes are acceptable when each SHARE has equal area. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.G.A.3. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original Activity1 learners FOLD rectangular paper into2/4/3 EQUAL-area pieces, CUT, label pieces and compare with partner. Unequal areas are not thirds even if three pieces exist. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: A half is ONE of2 equal-area pieces, a third ONE of3 equal-area pieces, and a fourth ONE of4 equal-area pieces. Different shapes are acceptable when each SHARE has equal area. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Activity1 learners FOLD rectangular paper into2/4/3 EQUAL-area pieces, CUT, label pieces and compare with partner. Unequal areas are not thirds even if three pieces exist. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Source warmup figures and real folds/cuts require human equality-of-area assessment, not piece count only.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. A half is ONE of2 equal-area pieces, a third ONE of3 equal-area pieces, and a fourth ONE of4 equal-area pieces. Different shapes are acceptable when each SHARE has equal area. Academic safeguard: Source warmup figures and real folds/cuts require human equality-of-area assessment, not piece count only.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson7: A half is ONE of2 equal-area pieces, a third ONE of3 equal-area pieces, and a fourth ONE of4 equal-area pieces. Different shapes are acceptable when each SHARE has equal area. Source warmup figures and real folds/cuts require human equality-of-area assessment, not piece count only."]),
        new(89,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L08","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-8/preparation.html","5fa6bc7c47b5fd908aa23cdd90eb004faaaebf98c4aa40f4080739bbdbd5cd64",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.3","CCSS:2.NBT.A.2"],
            ["Learning goal: You will understand that equal pieces of an identical whole do not need to be the same shape.\nIn previous lessons, you learned that when you partition circles and rectangles into halves, thirds, or fourths, each piece must be equal. To this point, you have determined whether the pieces are equal by cutting out pieces and physically matching them and by visually inspecting whether the pieces appear to be the same equal-size shape. In this lesson, you learn that halves, thirds, and fourths of the same whole can be different shapes or have different attributes.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Partition circles and rectangles into halves, thirds, and fourths in different ways\n- Recognize halves, thirds, and fourths of rectangles and circles\n- Use mathematical attributes such as sides, angles, parallelism, or length—not size or orientation—to justify a classification.",
             "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(25 - 15\\) \\(40 - 15\\) \\(65 - 25\\) \\(60 - 35\\)\nApproach: “What patterns did you notice when finding the value of these expressions?” (All the expressions were subtracting a number that had a 5 in the ones place. All the numbers are numbers we say when we count by 5, so we can count by 5 and 10 to find the values. When both numbers had a 5 in the ones place, I just subtracted the tens and knew there would be 0 ones. When only one number had a 5 in the ones place, I...\nExample 2: Lin wanted to partition this square into quarters. She started by splitting the square into halves. After she drew the first line, she tried 3 different ways to make fourths. Which of these shows fourths or quarters? Explain and share with your partner. A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C Name the shaded piece. A Caption: A Shape A has a ___ shaded. B...\nApproach: “Each of these you believe you have split the rectangle into fourths or quarters. Who do you agree with? Explain.” (The pieces look different in each rectangle, but you all show fourths because each rectangle is split into 4 equal pieces.)",
             "Step 1: Identify the figure and the attributes named in the task.\nStep 2: Measure, count, or compare sides, angles, corners, or other relevant features.\nStep 3: Use the attributes to classify, construct, or reason about the figure.\nStep 4: Explain which attributes support the result.\nStep 5: Check that the conclusion does not depend only on size or orientation.",
             "Common mistake: classifying a figure by appearance, size, or orientation. Use its mathematical attributes instead.",
             "You will understand that equal pieces of an identical whole do not need to be the same shape. In previous lessons, you learned that when you partition circles and rectangles into halves, thirds, or fourths, each piece must be equal."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson8 Are All Pieces Created Equal?. Partitioning into two/four equal AREA shares means halves/fourths; shape or orientation of parts can differ. Four pieces not equal area are not fourths. A whole contains4 fourths or2 halves. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original Number Talk25−15=10,40−15=25,65−25=40,60−35=25. Real publisher Lin's square quarter drawings A/B/C and shaded rectangles with possible multiple visual interpretations require actual source images. Educator reviews original unequal/equal area shapes and authentic student explanations, including alternative valid partitions.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: Partitioning into two/four equal AREA shares means halves/fourths; shape or orientation of parts can differ. Four pieces not equal area are not fourths. A whole contains4 fourths or2 halves. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.G.A.3, CCSS:2.NBT.A.2. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original Number Talk25−15=10,40−15=25,65−25=40,60−35=25. Real publisher Lin's square quarter drawings A/B/C and shaded rectangles with possible multiple visual interpretations require actual source images. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: Partitioning into two/four equal AREA shares means halves/fourths; shape or orientation of parts can differ. Four pieces not equal area are not fourths. A whole contains4 fourths or2 halves. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Number Talk25−15=10,40−15=25,65−25=40,60−35=25. Real publisher Lin's square quarter drawings A/B/C and shaded rectangles with possible multiple visual interpretations require actual source images. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Educator reviews original unequal/equal area shapes and authentic student explanations, including alternative valid partitions.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. Partitioning into two/four equal AREA shares means halves/fourths; shape or orientation of parts can differ. Four pieces not equal area are not fourths. A whole contains4 fourths or2 halves. Academic safeguard: Educator reviews original unequal/equal area shapes and authentic student explanations, including alternative valid partitions.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson8: Partitioning into two/four equal AREA shares means halves/fourths; shape or orientation of parts can differ. Four pieces not equal area are not fourths. A whole contains4 fourths or2 halves. Educator reviews original unequal/equal area shapes and authentic student explanations, including alternative valid partitions."]),
        new(90,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L09","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-9/preparation.html","ba42dfc57607015de8bf0bb2898979dafbc2519a1f0fa8347f4b8b2a14b6cddc",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.3","CCSS:2.NBT.A.2"],
            ["Learning goal: You will recognize 2 halves, 3 thirds, and 4 fourths as one whole.\nIn previous lessons, you partitioned circles and rectangles into halves, thirds, and fourths and identified an equal piece of different shapes as a half of, third, of, or fourth of the shape. In this lesson, you continue to practice partitioning circles and describe halves, thirds, and quarters of circles using the language a half of, a third of, and a quarter of to describe a piece of the shape. You also use this language to describe the whole shape as a number of equal pieces.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Describe two halves, three thirds, and four fourths as one whole\n- Use “half of,” “a third of,” and “a quarter of” to describe parts of a shape\n- Use mathematical attributes such as sides, angles, parallelism, or length—not size or orientation—to justify a classification.",
             "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(20 + 10 + 10 + 5\\) \\(30 + 25\\) \\(35 + 15\\) \\(15 + 25 + 15\\)\nApproach: “How could the second problem help you think about the last one?” (I know that \\(15 + 15 = 30\\) , so it was the same problem.)\nExample 2: Clare’s friends were going to share a pizza. The image shows how they cut the pizza. Clare ate 3 slices and her friends got upset with her. Why are her friends upset? How many thirds did Clare eat? How much of the pizza was left? Pizza Parts Group Priya Han Diego Priya will eat ___ of the pizza. Together they will eat ___ of the pizza. Pizza Parts Group...\nApproach: “Each group had the same-size pizza. Of all the you, which group of you will have the largest slices? Why do you think that is?” (Jada and Mai only have to share the pizza with 2 people, so you have the largest pieces.) “What do you notice about the size of the slices and the number of you?” (The slices get smaller if there are more you.)",
             "Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.",
             "Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.",
             "You will recognize 2 halves, 3 thirds, and 4 fourths as one whole. In previous lessons, you partitioned circles and rectangles into halves, thirds, and fourths and identified an equal piece of different shapes as a half of, third, of, or fourth of the shape."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson9 You Ate the Whole Thing. One whole equals2 halves,3 thirds,4 fourths. With equal whole pizzas, smaller denominators mean larger equal pieces (one half larger than one fourth). Three thirds is the ENTIRE pizza. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original Number Talk20+10+10+5=45,30+25=55,35+15=50,15+25+15=55. Pizza figure: Clare ate3 slices of a pizza divided into thirds, so none remains. Other groups Priya/Han/Diego and Jada/Mai compare actual fair shares. Teacher must inspect actual pizza partition drawings and real shading; never infer size of publisher image slices from a text-only summary.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: One whole equals2 halves,3 thirds,4 fourths. With equal whole pizzas, smaller denominators mean larger equal pieces (one half larger than one fourth). Three thirds is the ENTIRE pizza. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.G.A.3, CCSS:2.NBT.A.2. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original Number Talk20+10+10+5=45,30+25=55,35+15=50,15+25+15=55. Pizza figure: Clare ate3 slices of a pizza divided into thirds, so none remains. Other groups Priya/Han/Diego and Jada/Mai compare actual fair shares. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: One whole equals2 halves,3 thirds,4 fourths. With equal whole pizzas, smaller denominators mean larger equal pieces (one half larger than one fourth). Three thirds is the ENTIRE pizza. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Number Talk20+10+10+5=45,30+25=55,35+15=50,15+25+15=55. Pizza figure: Clare ate3 slices of a pizza divided into thirds, so none remains. Other groups Priya/Han/Diego and Jada/Mai compare actual fair shares. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Teacher must inspect actual pizza partition drawings and real shading; never infer size of publisher image slices from a text-only summary.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. One whole equals2 halves,3 thirds,4 fourths. With equal whole pizzas, smaller denominators mean larger equal pieces (one half larger than one fourth). Three thirds is the ENTIRE pizza. Academic safeguard: Teacher must inspect actual pizza partition drawings and real shading; never infer size of publisher image slices from a text-only summary.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson9: One whole equals2 halves,3 thirds,4 fourths. With equal whole pizzas, smaller denominators mean larger equal pieces (one half larger than one fourth). Three thirds is the ENTIRE pizza. Teacher must inspect actual pizza partition drawings and real shading; never infer size of publisher image slices from a text-only summary."]),
        new(91,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L10","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-10/preparation.html","7bb2cc74e1340d10f4e30b8c3ebfb18d8e5acc149f691416b532858e5db1e168",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.A.1"],
            ["Learning goal: You will work with shapes.\nThis lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need. In Activity 1, you learn stage 2 of the How Are You the Same? center, which was first introduced in grade 1. In this new stage, called Grade 2 Shapes, you develop your understanding of shapes by finding shared attributes.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Describe shapes using defining attributes\n- Draw and name shapes based on defining attributes\n- Choose a representation that matches the quantities and relationship, then use it to justify and check the result.",
             "Example 1: How many do you see? How do you see them?\nApproach: “Let’s say the value represented by the images in order.” (25, 50, 75, 100) “What pattern did you notice when combining the groups of the blocks and the value the blocks represent?” (When there were 2 groups of 25 it made a new ten with no ones left over, but when it was 3 groups of 25 there was a new ten and an extra 5.)",
             "Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.",
             "Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.",
             "You will work with shapes. This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson10 Center Day 2 (optional). Optional center day reuses fraction/shape activity and number-value groupings; four original source block figures count25,50,75,100. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original hands-on centers involve real block or shape materials selected by class and partnerships; no source player scores provided. Actual optional centers and original images remain teacher observed; generated numeric questions only companion.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: Optional center day reuses fraction/shape activity and number-value groupings; four original source block figures count25,50,75,100. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.G.A.1, CCSS:2.NBT.A.1. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original hands-on centers involve real block or shape materials selected by class and partnerships; no source player scores provided. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: Optional center day reuses fraction/shape activity and number-value groupings; four original source block figures count25,50,75,100. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original hands-on centers involve real block or shape materials selected by class and partnerships; no source player scores provided. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Actual optional centers and original images remain teacher observed; generated numeric questions only companion.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. Optional center day reuses fraction/shape activity and number-value groupings; four original source block figures count25,50,75,100. Academic safeguard: Actual optional centers and original images remain teacher observed; generated numeric questions only companion.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson10: Optional center day reuses fraction/shape activity and number-value groupings; four original source block figures count25,50,75,100. Actual optional centers and original images remain teacher observed; generated numeric questions only companion."]),
        new(92,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L11","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-11/preparation.html","4589c38566e6315f522f077dcf46066fb4574e2cb231b0869c5112c78e4d3313",
            ["CCSS:2.MD.C.7"],
            ["Learning goal: You will tell time with an analog clock using the words half past, quarter past, and quarter till.\nIn grade 1, you learned to tell and write time to the hour and half-hour. You related representations of time to the half-hour on analog clocks to circles partitioned into halves. In previous lessons, you partitioned shapes, including circles, into halves and quarters.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Tell time from an analog clock using the words half past, quarter past, and quarter till\n- In grade 1, you learned to tell and write time to the hour and half-hour\n- Read the hour and minute hands separately; for hour and half-hour times, :00 and :30 must match the minute-hand position.",
             "Example 1: Circle the clock that shows 4 o’clock. Why doesn’t the other clock show 4 o’clock? Circle the clock that shows half past 7. Why doesn’t the other clock show half past 7? Draw the hands on the clock to show 10:00. Draw the hands on the clock to show 1:30.\nApproach: “Tyler and Mai both draw hands on the clock to show 1:30. Which clock shows 1:30? Explain.” (Tyler’s clock shows 1:30 because he drew the hour hand halfway between the 1 and the 2. The minute hand points to 6. It has traveled halfway around the clock, so the hour hand has traveled halfway to the next hour.)\nExample 2: Find matching sets of cards. Each set should have 3 cards. Be prepared to explain why they match. Write the time shown on each clock using the words half past, quarter past, or quarter till.\nApproach: “Look at the hour hand on these 3 clocks. What do you notice about how it moves as the minute hand moves?” (The hour hand moves away from the 5 and closer to the 6.)",
             "Step 1: Identify whether the task asks for an hour or half-hour time.\nStep 2: Read the hour hand first.\nStep 3: Read the minute hand and connect 12 with :00 or 6 with :30 when appropriate.\nStep 4: Write or select the matching time.\nStep 5: Check that both hands agree with the written time.",
             "Common mistake: mixing up the hour and minute hands. Check each hand separately before writing the time.",
             "You will tell time with an analog clock using the words half past, quarter past, and quarter till. In grade 1, you learned to tell and write time to the hour and half-hour."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson11 Tell Time with Halves and Quarters. Analog time has hour AND minute hands. Minute hand on12 means:00; on3 means:15 (quarter past), on6 means:30 (half past), on9 means:45 (quarter to). At7:30 hour hand lies between7 and8. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original Activity1 find authentic 4:00 versus wrong clock, half past7 versus wrong clock; DRAW hands for10:00 and1:30. Activity2 sort genuine THREE-CARD clock and time sets and say half past, quarter past/to. Educator checks exact hand angle/position, actual publisher clock images, card matching and written words.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: Analog time has hour AND minute hands. Minute hand on12 means:00; on3 means:15 (quarter past), on6 means:30 (half past), on9 means:45 (quarter to). At7:30 hour hand lies between7 and8. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.MD.C.7. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original Activity1 find authentic 4:00 versus wrong clock, half past7 versus wrong clock; DRAW hands for10:00 and1:30. Activity2 sort genuine THREE-CARD clock and time sets and say half past, quarter past/to. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: Analog time has hour AND minute hands. Minute hand on12 means:00; on3 means:15 (quarter past), on6 means:30 (half past), on9 means:45 (quarter to). At7:30 hour hand lies between7 and8. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Activity1 find authentic 4:00 versus wrong clock, half past7 versus wrong clock; DRAW hands for10:00 and1:30. Activity2 sort genuine THREE-CARD clock and time sets and say half past, quarter past/to. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Educator checks exact hand angle/position, actual publisher clock images, card matching and written words.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. Analog time has hour AND minute hands. Minute hand on12 means:00; on3 means:15 (quarter past), on6 means:30 (half past), on9 means:45 (quarter to). At7:30 hour hand lies between7 and8. Academic safeguard: Educator checks exact hand angle/position, actual publisher clock images, card matching and written words.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson11: Analog time has hour AND minute hands. Minute hand on12 means:00; on3 means:15 (quarter past), on6 means:30 (half past), on9 means:45 (quarter to). At7:30 hour hand lies between7 and8. Educator checks exact hand angle/position, actual publisher clock images, card matching and written words."]),
        new(93,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L12","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-12/preparation.html","2605459d6716c8526aed3d05f7343c30ef8d5b14a5c1254482e1b4120f3da3e5",
            ["CCSS:2.MD.C.7","CCSS:2.NBT.A.2"],
            ["Learning goal: You will tell time to the nearest 5 minutes on an analog clock.\nIn grade 1, you learned to tell time to the hour and half-hour. In a previous lesson, you learned to tell time using the phrases “half past,” “quarter past,” and “quarter till,” and described the relative position of the hour hand. In this lesson, you make connections between a number line with intervals of 5 and the distance between the numbers on the clock, which represents 5 minute intervals.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Tell time on analog clocks\n- Understand that the numbers on an analog clock represent 5-minute intervals\n- Read the hour and minute hands separately; for hour and half-hour times, :00 and :30 must match the minute-hand position.",
             "Example 1: Discuss 2 ways to read the time on this clock. What time does this clock show? Read the time on each clock card with your partner. Put the clocks in order based on the times they show.\nApproach: “What do you notice about the hour hand when you look at the clocks in order?” (It moves a little toward the next hour each time. It is getting closer and closer to 9.)\nExample 2: Write the time shown on each clock.\nApproach: “How did ___ know you could start counting at 30? Why does this work?” (You know it is 2:30 when the minute hand points to 6, so you can just start there. It works because you will still count the same numbers. If you start at 30 you still say 35, 40. It is just faster.) “Which is the correct way to show the time on the clock using digits? Explain.” (3:05 because it’s five minutes after 3. You have to use 2...",
             "Step 1: Identify whether the task asks for an hour or half-hour time.\nStep 2: Read the hour hand first.\nStep 3: Read the minute hand and connect 12 with :00 or 6 with :30 when appropriate.\nStep 4: Write or select the matching time.\nStep 5: Check that both hands agree with the written time.",
             "Common mistake: mixing up the hour and minute hands. Check each hand separately before writing the time.",
             "You will tell time to the nearest 5 minutes on an analog clock. In grade 1, you learned to tell time to the hour and half-hour."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson12 Count by 5 to Tell Time. Each printed clock number is a jump of5 minutes. Minute hand on1 means05, on6 means30, on8 means40, on11 means55; advancing minutes moves hour hand gradually toward next hour. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original Activity1 compare TWO ways to read one actual clock, organize publisher analog CLOCK CARDS by chronological time, talk about the hour hand approaching9. Activity2 write displayed source clock times, including3:05 with leading0 minutes. Teacher checks actual visible hands and clock cards, source chronological ordering and student readings; image-only time not guessed.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: Each printed clock number is a jump of5 minutes. Minute hand on1 means05, on6 means30, on8 means40, on11 means55; advancing minutes moves hour hand gradually toward next hour. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.MD.C.7, CCSS:2.NBT.A.2. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original Activity1 compare TWO ways to read one actual clock, organize publisher analog CLOCK CARDS by chronological time, talk about the hour hand approaching9. Activity2 write displayed source clock times, including3:05 with leading0 minutes. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: Each printed clock number is a jump of5 minutes. Minute hand on1 means05, on6 means30, on8 means40, on11 means55; advancing minutes moves hour hand gradually toward next hour. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Activity1 compare TWO ways to read one actual clock, organize publisher analog CLOCK CARDS by chronological time, talk about the hour hand approaching9. Activity2 write displayed source clock times, including3:05 with leading0 minutes. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Teacher checks actual visible hands and clock cards, source chronological ordering and student readings; image-only time not guessed.",
