             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson12: Each printed clock number is a jump of5 minutes. Minute hand on1 means05, on6 means30, on8 means40, on11 means55; advancing minutes moves hour hand gradually toward next hour. Teacher checks actual visible hands and clock cards, source chronological ordering and student readings; image-only time not guessed."]),
        new(94,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L13","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-13/preparation.html","e25904616e213f64c364270d427326e89f1ec5e9d7b20ef387573699c5a17eb2",
            ["CCSS:2.MD.C.7"],
            ["Learning goal: You will read and write time with analog and digital clocks, using a.\nIn previous lessons, you learned to tell time to the nearest 5 minutes using analog clocks. In this lesson, you practice telling time to the nearest 5 minutes. You recognize that the hour hand goes around the clock twice each day, so a.m. and p.m. are used to distinguish between morning and night.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Label times using a.m. and p.m\n- Read and write time to the nearest 5-minute interval on analog and digital clocks\n- Read the hour and minute hands separately; for hour and half-hour times, :00 and :30 must match the minute-hand position.",
             "Example 1: Label each activity with a.m. or p.m. Draw a line to the time when the activity could take place. Draw the hands on the clock to show the time. activity do homework ___ get ready for bed ___ eat lunch ___ on the way to school ___ in bed sleeping ___ time\nApproach: “Explain your reasoning for your answer.” (2:00 p.m. because 2 a.m. is in the middle of the night. Most people would not walk your dog in the middle of the night.)",
             "Step 1: Identify whether the task asks for an hour or half-hour time.\nStep 2: Read the hour hand first.\nStep 3: Read the minute hand and connect 12 with :00 or 6 with :30 when appropriate.\nStep 4: Write or select the matching time.\nStep 5: Check that both hands agree with the written time.",
             "Common mistake: mixing up the hour and minute hands. Check each hand separately before writing the time.",
             "You will read and write time with analog and digital clocks, using a. In previous lessons, you learned to tell time to the nearest 5 minutes using analog clocks."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson13 Is It a.m. or p.m.? PLC Activity. a.m. is the time from midnight before noon, p.m. from noon until midnight. The SAME 2:00 analog clock may represent2 a.m. or2 p.m.; context alone can give several plausible daily schedules. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original students label do homework, get ready for bed, eat lunch, go to school, sleep with a.m. or p.m., CONNECT to plausible original publisher clock and DRAW hands. Valid family routines vary; avoid fixed universal time grading. Teacher reviews personal schedule context, original image labels and plausible alternate time arrangements.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: a.m. is the time from midnight before noon, p.m. from noon until midnight. The SAME 2:00 analog clock may represent2 a.m. or2 p.m.; context alone can give several plausible daily schedules. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.MD.C.7. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original students label do homework, get ready for bed, eat lunch, go to school, sleep with a.m. or p.m., CONNECT to plausible original publisher clock and DRAW hands. Valid family routines vary; avoid fixed universal time grading. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: a.m. is the time from midnight before noon, p.m. from noon until midnight. The SAME 2:00 analog clock may represent2 a.m. or2 p.m.; context alone can give several plausible daily schedules. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original students label do homework, get ready for bed, eat lunch, go to school, sleep with a.m. or p.m., CONNECT to plausible original publisher clock and DRAW hands. Valid family routines vary; avoid fixed universal time grading. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Teacher reviews personal schedule context, original image labels and plausible alternate time arrangements.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. a.m. is the time from midnight before noon, p.m. from noon until midnight. The SAME 2:00 analog clock may represent2 a.m. or2 p.m.; context alone can give several plausible daily schedules. Academic safeguard: Teacher reviews personal schedule context, original image labels and plausible alternate time arrangements.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson13: a.m. is the time from midnight before noon, p.m. from noon until midnight. The SAME 2:00 analog clock may represent2 a.m. or2 p.m.; context alone can give several plausible daily schedules. Teacher reviews personal schedule context, original image labels and plausible alternate time arrangements."]),
        new(95,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L14","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-14/preparation.html","30dd24f948a4120abc5fce65567b06109e54ccb1476f2a1cdcca982364f5b68e",
            ["CCSS:2.G.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.NBT.B.6"],
            ["Learning goal: You will recognize shapes, describe the attributes of shapes, and practice adding and subtracting within 100.\nThis lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need. In Activity 1, you revisit stage 3 of the Picture Books center. This center was first introduced in Kindergarten, and this stage in grade 1.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Practice addition and subtraction within 100\n- Recognize and describe shapes seen in picture books\n- Choose a representation that matches the quantities and relationship, then use it to justify and check the result.",
             "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(10 + 15 + 20 + 10\\) \\(5 + 30 + 20\\) \\(15 + 20 + 30 \\) \\(25 + 15 + 5 + 15\\)\nApproach: Represent the quantities or relationships clearly, apply the lesson idea, and check that the result answers the question.",
             "Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.",
             "Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.",
             "You will recognize shapes, describe the attributes of shapes, and practice adding and subtracting within 100. This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson14 Center Day 3 (optional). Optional centers review shapes and mental addition. Source warmup10+15+20+10=55,5+30+20=55,15+20+30=65,25+15+5+15=60. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Students select and play source centers using real physical diagrams and available number/shape tasks, not an automatic online replacement. Original optional center choices and genuine participant drawings teacher-reviewed.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: Optional centers review shapes and mental addition. Source warmup10+15+20+10=55,5+30+20=55,15+20+30=65,25+15+5+15=60. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.G.A.1, CCSS:2.NBT.B.5, CCSS:2.NBT.B.6. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Students select and play source centers using real physical diagrams and available number/shape tasks, not an automatic online replacement. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: Optional centers review shapes and mental addition. Source warmup10+15+20+10=55,5+30+20=55,15+20+30=65,25+15+5+15=60. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Students select and play source centers using real physical diagrams and available number/shape tasks, not an automatic online replacement. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Original optional center choices and genuine participant drawings teacher-reviewed.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. Optional centers review shapes and mental addition. Source warmup10+15+20+10=55,5+30+20=55,15+20+30=65,25+15+5+15=60. Academic safeguard: Original optional center choices and genuine participant drawings teacher-reviewed.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson14: Optional centers review shapes and mental addition. Source warmup10+15+20+10=55,5+30+20=55,15+20+30=65,25+15+5+15=60. Original optional center choices and genuine participant drawings teacher-reviewed."]),
        new(96,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L15","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-15/preparation.html","5d48b72bb8e11dcaa1c4ef923fe6d489e8763eb542b7054acae6e6e98a91ff41",
            ["CCSS:2.MD.C.8","CCSS:2.NBT.A.2","CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.NBT.B.6","CCSS:2.NBT.B.8"],
            ["Learning goal: You will identify pennies, nickels, and dimes and find the total value of a set of coins.\nIn previous units, you learned to use a ten when adding or subtracting within 100. You also used counting on or counting back as a strategy when adding or subtracting, including counting on or counting back by 5 and 10. In this lesson, you recognize dimes, nickels, and pennies and learn your values.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Identify pennies, nickels, and dimes and know your values\n- Use skip counting and counting on by 5 and 10 to find the value of a set of coins\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.",
             "Example 1: Name the coins in each group and find the value in cents. Show your thinking using numbers, words, drawings, or equations. Andre’s coins: Circle the name of the coins in this collection: dimes nickels pennies What is the value of the coins? Clare’s coins: Circle the name of the coins in this collection: dimes nickels pennies What is the value of the...\nApproach: “What do you notice about Han’s coins?” (You’re nickels. You’re all the same size. Some show a head and some show a building. One side says “five cents.”)\nExample 2: Name the coins in each group and find the value in cents. Show your thinking using numbers, words, drawings, or equations. Mai’s coins: Circle the names of the coins in this collection: dimes nickels pennies What is the value of the coins? Andre’s coins: Circle the names of the coins in this collection: dimes nickels pennies What is the value of the...\nApproach: Represent the quantities, write a matching addition relationship, then combine or count on to find and check the total.",
             "Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.",
             "Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.",
             "You will identify pennies, nickels, and dimes and find the total value of a set of coins. In previous units, you learned to use a ten when adding or subtracting within 100."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson15 Identify Pennies, Nickels, and Dimes. US coin values: penny1 cent, nickel5 cents, dime10 cents. Coin size/side appearance does NOT determine value; heads and tails of a nickel remain5 cents. Total is1p+5n+10d. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original publisher shows Andre, Clare, Han COIN images (some showing faces, others reverse) and Activity2 Mai/Andre collections. Learner identifies true coins and totals using genuine photographs and counting. Actual original coin photos and quantities cannot be inferred from generic descriptions; teacher checks source figures.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: US coin values: penny1 cent, nickel5 cents, dime10 cents. Coin size/side appearance does NOT determine value; heads and tails of a nickel remain5 cents. Total is1p+5n+10d. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.MD.C.8, CCSS:2.NBT.A.2, CCSS:2.NBT.B.5, CCSS:2.NBT.B.6, CCSS:2.NBT.B.8. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original publisher shows Andre, Clare, Han COIN images (some showing faces, others reverse) and Activity2 Mai/Andre collections. Learner identifies true coins and totals using genuine photographs and counting. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: US coin values: penny1 cent, nickel5 cents, dime10 cents. Coin size/side appearance does NOT determine value; heads and tails of a nickel remain5 cents. Total is1p+5n+10d. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original publisher shows Andre, Clare, Han COIN images (some showing faces, others reverse) and Activity2 Mai/Andre collections. Learner identifies true coins and totals using genuine photographs and counting. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Actual original coin photos and quantities cannot be inferred from generic descriptions; teacher checks source figures.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. US coin values: penny1 cent, nickel5 cents, dime10 cents. Coin size/side appearance does NOT determine value; heads and tails of a nickel remain5 cents. Total is1p+5n+10d. Academic safeguard: Actual original coin photos and quantities cannot be inferred from generic descriptions; teacher checks source figures.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson15: US coin values: penny1 cent, nickel5 cents, dime10 cents. Coin size/side appearance does NOT determine value; heads and tails of a nickel remain5 cents. Total is1p+5n+10d. Actual original coin photos and quantities cannot be inferred from generic descriptions; teacher checks source figures."]),
        new(97,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L16","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-16/preparation.html","e932f98fe77b9468b727f303ca4daf5e704be37d30bf43f7b4bf2d115edecb39",
            ["CCSS:2.MD.C.8","CCSS:2.NBT.A.2","CCSS:2.NBT.B.5"],
            ["Learning goal: You will identify quarters and find the total value of a set of coins including quarters.\nIn a previous lesson, you recognized dimes, nickels, and pennies and learned your values. You used different strategies to find the value of a mixed set of coins. In this lesson, you recognize the quarter and learn its value.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Find the value of a set of coins including all combinations\n- Identify and know the value of quarters\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.",
             "Example 1: Write the names and values of the coins you know. Complete the table so each row shows a value in cents and two different groups of coins that have that value.\nApproach: Represent the quantities, write a matching addition relationship, then combine or count on to find and check the total.\nExample 2: Elena's Coins Elena has: quarters dimes nickels pennies How many cents? Show your thinking. Tyler's Coins Tyler has: quarters dimes nickels pennies How many cents? Show your thinking. Make your own set of coins with a value of 97¢. I used: quarters dimes nickels pennies Make your own set of coins with a value of 66¢. Use the fewest number of coins...\nApproach: Represent the quantities, write a matching addition relationship, then combine or count on to find and check the total.",
             "Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.",
             "Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.",
             "You will identify quarters and find the total value of a set of coins including quarters. In a previous lesson, you recognized dimes, nickels, and pennies and learned your values."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson16 Identify Quarters PLC Activity. A quarter equals25 cents; penny1, nickel5, dime10. Counts p,n,d,q give cents=p+5n+10d+25q. Several different arrangements can have same cents total. The fewest coins for66 cents with US denominations25/10/5/1 is two quarters, one dime, one nickel, one penny (5 coins). SOURCE REAL TASKS: Publisher first task builds TWO distinct coin collections for each given amount. Original Elena/Tyler coin image counts unknown in text. Student invents collection97 cents, and minimum-coins66 cents. Teacher checks actual original quarter appearance, shaded/countable image coins and student constructed alternate solutions; correct combinations more than one.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: A quarter equals25 cents; penny1, nickel5, dime10. Counts p,n,d,q give cents=p+5n+10d+25q. Several different arrangements can have same cents total. The fewest coins for66 cents with US denominations25/10/5/1 is two quarters, one dime, one nickel, one penny (5 coins). Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.MD.C.8, CCSS:2.NBT.A.2, CCSS:2.NBT.B.5. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Publisher first task builds TWO distinct coin collections for each given amount. Original Elena/Tyler coin image counts unknown in text. Student invents collection97 cents, and minimum-coins66 cents. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: A quarter equals25 cents; penny1, nickel5, dime10. Counts p,n,d,q give cents=p+5n+10d+25q. Several different arrangements can have same cents total. The fewest coins for66 cents with US denominations25/10/5/1 is two quarters, one dime, one nickel, one penny (5 coins). 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Publisher first task builds TWO distinct coin collections for each given amount. Original Elena/Tyler coin image counts unknown in text. Student invents collection97 cents, and minimum-coins66 cents. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Teacher checks actual original quarter appearance, shaded/countable image coins and student constructed alternate solutions; correct combinations more than one.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. A quarter equals25 cents; penny1, nickel5, dime10. Counts p,n,d,q give cents=p+5n+10d+25q. Several different arrangements can have same cents total. The fewest coins for66 cents with US denominations25/10/5/1 is two quarters, one dime, one nickel, one penny (5 coins). Academic safeguard: Teacher checks actual original quarter appearance, shaded/countable image coins and student constructed alternate solutions; correct combinations more than one.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson16: A quarter equals25 cents; penny1, nickel5, dime10. Counts p,n,d,q give cents=p+5n+10d+25q. Several different arrangements can have same cents total. The fewest coins for66 cents with US denominations25/10/5/1 is two quarters, one dime, one nickel, one penny (5 coins). Teacher checks actual original quarter appearance, shaded/countable image coins and student constructed alternate solutions; correct combinations more than one."]),
        new(98,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L17","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-17/preparation.html","0dba45c55c637e5546d77fba549fd98f35bae28d79b61243d38ac62abf3dc96c",
            ["CCSS:2.MD.C.8","CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.NBT.B.6"],
            ["Learning goal: You will add and subtract within 100 to find values of100 cents and recognize a dollar as the same value as 100 cents.\nIn previous lessons, you learned the values of quarters, dimes, nickels, and pennies. You used strategies for adding within 100 to find the total value of a set of coins and found different combinations of coins that have the same value. In this lesson, you find different combinations of coins that have a value of 100 cents.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Find combinations of coins that have a value of 100 cents\n- Understand that a dollar has the same value as 100 cents\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.",
             "Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(25 + 10 + 10 + 5\\) \\(25 + 25\\) \\(25 + 25 + 25\\) \\(25 + 25 + 25 + 25\\)\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.\nExample 2: Coin Collection A Circle the coins in this collection. quarters dimes nickels pennies How many coins? ___ What is the value in cents? Show your thinking. Coin Collection B Circle the coins in this collection. quarters dimes nickels pennies How many coins? ___ What is the value in cents? Show your thinking. Coin Collection C Make a collection using only...\nApproach: “How could you use what you know about nickels to figure out how many dimes you needed?” (For every 2 nickels we needed 1 dime.) “How many pennies would you need in order to have the same value? Explain.” (100 because a penny is worth 1 cent, so you need 100 to make 100 cents.)",
             "Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.",
             "Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.",
             "You will add and subtract within 100 to find values of100 cents and recognize a dollar as the same value as 100 cents. In previous lessons, you learned the values of quarters, dimes, nickels, and pennies."],
            ["IM2021 G2 Unit6 Lesson17 Let’s Make a Dollar. One US dollar=100 cents=4 quarters=10 dimes=20 nickels=100 pennies. Changing coin denominations does not change the total. To complete a dollar from a known collection subtract current value from100. SOURCE REAL TASKS: Original Number Talk25+10+10+5=50,25+25=50,25+25+25=75,4 quarters=100. Source Activity1 original A/B coin collections with figures; Activity2 learners make different true combinations using ONLY dimes/nickels/pennies or quarters as specified in original paper. Educator checks original actual figure quantities and multiple valid pupil-made coin sets; no imaginary publisher collection.",
             "Lesson-specific rules: One US dollar=100 cents=4 quarters=10 dimes=20 nickels=100 pennies. Changing coin denominations does not change the total. To complete a dollar from a known collection subtract current value from100. Official source CCSS outcome(s): CCSS:2.MD.C.8, CCSS:2.NBT.B.5, CCSS:2.NBT.B.6. Compare/construct original TASK, not generic unrelated arithmetic.",
             "ORIGINAL publisher public student-facing task evidence: Original Number Talk25+10+10+5=50,25+25=50,25+25+25=75,4 quarters=100. Source Activity1 original A/B coin collections with figures; Activity2 learners make different true combinations using ONLY dimes/nickels/pennies or quarters as specified in original paper. Genuine original images or classroom drawings need teacher judgement; no inaccessible publisher Cool-down reconstructed. FIRST-PARTY Edulytics question families use newly generated synthetic values and are not original source answer sheets.",
             "1. Reproduce mathematically relevant ORIGINAL warm-up and source lesson ideas without guessing figure details: One US dollar=100 cents=4 quarters=10 dimes=20 nickels=100 pennies. Changing coin denominations does not change the total. To complete a dollar from a known collection subtract current value from100. 2. Read the actual original activity requirements: Original Number Talk25+10+10+5=50,25+25=50,25+25+25=75,4 quarters=100. Source Activity1 original A/B coin collections with figures; Activity2 learners make different true combinations using ONLY dimes/nickels/pennies or quarters as specified in original paper. 3. Complete source-appropriate geometric, partition, time or coin representation with correct units and reasoning. 4. For numerical evidence, record authentic given values, and distinguish given values from student-produced outcomes. 5. Use a separate first-party source-aligned numerical practice family with exact verification; creative/visual/drawing tasks need educator. 6. Verify source limitations explicitly: Educator checks original actual figure quantities and multiple valid pupil-made coin sets; no imaginary publisher collection.",
             "Likely mistake: use a generic equation while ignoring the defining attribute, equal area, clock minute-hand mark or coin denomination. One US dollar=100 cents=4 quarters=10 dimes=20 nickels=100 pennies. Changing coin denominations does not change the total. To complete a dollar from a known collection subtract current value from100. Academic safeguard: Educator checks original actual figure quantities and multiple valid pupil-made coin sets; no imaginary publisher collection.",
             "Grade2 Unit6 Lesson17: One US dollar=100 cents=4 quarters=10 dimes=20 nickels=100 pennies. Changing coin denominations does not change the total. To complete a dollar from a known collection subtract current value from100. Educator checks original actual figure quantities and multiple valid pupil-made coin sets; no imaginary publisher collection."]),
        new(99,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U06:L18","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-6/lesson-18/preparation.html","5ae950269317f0614c6f970242c2014bdb34a8d8670aff26af4ff2ad6b9c0331",
            ["CCSS:2.MD.C.8","CCSS:2.NBT.B.5","CCSS:2.OA.A.1"],
            ["Learning goal: You will use addition and subtraction to solve story problems in the context of money.\nIn previous lessons, you used addition to find the total value of a set of coins and identified 100 cents as a dollar. In this lesson, you solve story problems in the context of money. In the first activity, you continue practicing finding the value of a collection of coins and use the coins and your total value to solve problems in the context of a class store.",
             "Key ideas:\n- Solve addition and subtraction story problems in the context of money\n- In previous lessons, you used addition to find the total value of a set of coins and identified 100 cents as a dollar\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.",
             "Example 1: How many coins do you see? How do you see them?\nApproach: “All of these images had a total of 8 coins. Does that mean you all have the same value? Explain.” (Each of the coins are worth different amounts, so the values are different. 8¢, 40¢, and 80¢)\nExample 2: items cost pack of pencils 75¢ pencil sharpener 35¢ eraser 45¢ pens 18¢ Lin has these coins: How much money does Lin have for supplies? If Lin buys an eraser, how much money will she have left? Show your thinking. Diego has these coins: How much money does Diego have for supplies? If Diego buys a pack of pencils, how much money will he have left? Show...\nApproach: “When did you use addition in the problems? When did you subtract? Why?” (When I was finding how much money, I added. When you bought something, I had to subtract.)",
             "Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.",
             "Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.",
